{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\csv\\QA-Kaggle-dataset-2024_utf8.csv", "filename": "QA-Kaggle-dataset-2024_utf8.csv", "filepath": "data\\documents\\csv\\QA-Kaggle-dataset-2024_utf8.csv", "page": 8, "title": "QA-Kaggle-dataset-2024_utf8.csv", "year": "2024", "row": 7}, "page_content": "\ufeffWhat is psoriasis and what are its common symptoms?: What are some common treatments for acne?\nPsoriasis is a chronic autoimmune condition that results in the overproduction of skin cells. This overproduction leads to patches of thick, red skin covered with silvery scales. Common symptoms include red patches of skin covered with thick, silvery scales, small scaling spots (commonly seen in children), dry and cracked skin that may bleed, itching, burning, or soreness, thickened, pitted, or ridged nails, and swollen and stiff joints.: Treatment for acne depends on its severity and persistence. Mild acne can be treated with over-the-counter (OTC) medications, such as gels, creams, and lotions, that you apply to your skin. The active ingredients in these products may include salicylic acid, benzoyl peroxide, and resorcinol. \r\n\r\nFor moderate to severe acne, you may need a prescription for topical or oral medications. These could include antibiotics to help stop or slow the growth of bacteria and reduce inflammation, or retinoid creams, gels, lotions, or pills, which are vitamin A derivatives that regulate cell turnover to keep pores free of clogs.", "type": "Document"}}